<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="baicalein metabolism" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7212" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME:TAX-65409| (Baikal skullcap), a species of the &lt;i&gt;Lamiacea&lt;/i&gt; family, belongs to the 50 most important medicinal herbs used in traditional Chinese medicine. Especially the roots of this plant are rich on flavones which are characteristic tracer compounds in |FRAME:TAX-4139| plants. The flavone aglycones and glucuronides |FRAME:CPD-12725|, |FRAME:CPD-12724|, |FRAME:CPD-12728| and |FRAME:CPD-12727| (see |FRAME:PWY-7213|) are the most extensively studied flavones from |FRAME:TAX-4139| and have shown interesting pharmacological properties |CITS: [20708926]|. |FRAME:CPD-12724 Baicalein| is more effective than |FRAME:CPD-12725| and has demonstrated antibacterial |CITS:[7312987]|, antiviral |CITS:[1313232]| and anticancer |CITS:[9066718]| properties. In addition |FRAME:CPD-12724| was shown to stimulate the chloride secretion in marine vertebrates |CITS:[8390669]| and activates transcription factors involved in signaling pathways |CITS:[23339711]|. The metabolism of |FRAME:CPD-12724| comprises 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucurosylation |CITS:[10724177]|, deglucuronidation |CITS:[Morimoto95]|, |CITS:[8593473]|, |CITS: [10858442]| |CITS:[Zhang05]| and 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation |CITS:[10872235]| reactions. Notably, the baicalin glucurosidase will generate more |FRAME:CPD-12724|, which is usually present in |FRAME:TAX-4139| at much lower concentrations than |FRAME:CPD-12725| and might be a first plant defense response to deter pathogens |CITS:[20708926]|. Moreover, it appears that the baicalin glucurosidase reaction is involved in a novel |FRAME:HYDROGEN-PEROXIDE| detoxification pathway (see |FRAME:PWY-7214|).</Description>
